daykeep NEWS

* Noteworthy changes in release 0.1 (unreleased)

** New program

  daykeep works in decimal time: a day number plus a fraction of a day,
  counted from day 0 = 1987-07-28 00:00 UTC.  90 minutes is .0625.

  It sums START - END ranges from the operands, from -f FILE or from
  standard input, printing the running total as a decimal (the default),
  as hours and minutes (--hm) or as minutes (--format=minutes).

  A stamp may leave out digits.  Missing right digits are zeros, and
  missing left digits are completed to the most recent matching day (or
  the next one with a leading '+').  An END is completed from its START.
  HH:MM is read as local time, so old timekeep logs still sum.

  --now prints the current stamp, and --convert turns stamps into local
  dates and times and back.

  --track is an interactive tracker.  With -a LOG it keeps the session's
  entries in LOG, one range per line, so -f LOG reads them back.

** Packaging

  A man page (generated with help2man), a Texinfo manual that explains
  the calendar and the epoch, and bash completion.  'make dist' builds
  a tarball, and 'make distcheck' builds, tests, installs and uninstalls
  it.

** Verification

  'make verify' also checks daykeep's decimal time: Z3 proves the digit
  completion, range-end rollover and rounding rules over a model of the
  time library, and seeded property checks tie that model to the C.

** Fuzzing

  'make fuzz' runs seeded differential fuzzing of timekeep against its
  spec and of daykeep against a model of its decimal time, plus garbage
  input and random tracker sessions.  'make fuzz-asan' does the same with
  sanitizers, and 'make fuzz-libfuzzer' fuzzes the parsers under clang.

** Bug fixes found by fuzzing

  In --track, pressing Enter while the running entry's start was edited
  into the future wrote an entry that ended before it started, and the log
  no longer read back with -f.  The entry now ends at its start.

** Changes in timekeep

  timekeep is now a sum-only study program: the tracker mode moved to
  daykeep.  The hand-built ELF shrank from 2692 to 434 bytes and is still
  formally verified.
